H A COMMENTARY ON THE ELECTIONS.
h BY THE BEADLE OF SOMERSET HOUSE.
V Well, lawks-a-day! things seem going on uncommon queer,
V For they say that the Tories are bowling out the Whigs almost everywhere;
V And the blazing red of my beadle’s coat is turning to pink through fear,
V Lest I should find myself and staff out of Office some time about the end of
  the year.
V I’ve done nothing so long but stand under the magnificent portico
V Of Somerset House, that I don’t know what I should do if I was for to go!
V What the electors are at, I can’t make out, upon my soul,
V For it’s a law of natur’ that the __whig__ should be atop of the __poll__.
V I’ve had a snug berth of it here for some time, and don’t want to cut the
  connexion;
V But they __do__ say the Whigs must go out, because they’ve NO OTHER ELEC-
  TION;
V What they mean by that, I __don’t__ know, for ain’t they been electioneering—
V That is, they’ve been canvassing, and spouting, and pledging, and ginning,
  and beering.
V Hasn’t Crawford and Pattison, Lyall, Masterman, Wood, and Lord John
  Russell,
V For ever so long been keeping the Great Metropolis in one alarming
  __bussel__?
V Ain’t the two __first__ retired into private life—(that’s the genteel for being
  rejected)?
V And what’s more, the __last__ four, strange to say, have all been elected.
V Then Finsbury Tom and Mr. Wakley, as wears his hair all over his coat
  collar,
V Hav’n’t they frightened Mr. Tooke, who once said he could beat them
  __Hollar__?
V Then at Lambeth, ain’t Mr. Baldwin and Mr. Cabbell been both on ‘em
  bottled
V By Mr. D’Eyncourt and Mr. Hawes, who makes soap yellow and mottled!
V And hasn’t Sir Benjamin Hall, and the gallant Commodore Napier,
V Made such a cabal with Cabbell and Hamilton as would make any chap
  queer?
V Whilst Sankey, who was backed by a __Cleave__-r for Marrowbone looks
  cranky,
V Acos the electors, like lisping babbies, cried out “__No__ __Sankee?__”
V Then South’ark has sent Alderman Humphrey and Mr. B. Wood,
V Who has promised, that if ever a member of parliament did his duty—he
  would!
V Then for the Tower Hamlets, Robinson, Hutchinson, and Thompson, find
  that they’re in the wrong box,
V For the electors, though turned to Clay, still gallantly followed the Fox;
V Whilst Westminster’s chosen Rous—not Rouse of the Eagle—tho’ I once
  seed a
V Picture where there was a great big bird, very like a __goose__, along with a Leda.
V And hasn’t Sir Robert Peel and Mr. A’Court been down to Tamworth to
  be reseated?
V They ought to get an act of parliament to save them such fatigue, for its
  always—ditto repeated.
V Whilst at Leeds, Beckett and Aldam have put Lord Jocelyn into a consider-
  able fume,
V Who finds it no go, though he’s added up the poll-books several times
  with the calculating boy, Joe Hume.
V So if there’s been __no__ __other__ __election__, I should like to find out
V What all the late squibbing and fibbing, placarding, and blackguarding,
  losing and winning, beering and ginning, and every other __et__ __cetera__,
  has been about!
