H A NEW VERSION OF BELSHAZZAR’S FEAST.
@cut cut-66-1
# ? yyy
# ? [rr —$—$$—$}
C OLD GLORY’S WHIG TOP-BOOTS REFUSING TO CARRY HIM TO THE DINNER
  TO CAPTAIN ROUS.
P Such, we are credibly assured, was the determination of these
  liberal and enlightened leathers. They had heard frequent whis-
  pers of a general indisposition on the part of all lovers of consistency
  to stand in their master’s shoes, and taking the insult to themselves,
  they lately came to the resolution of cutting the connexion. They felt
  that his liberality and his boots were all that constituted the idea of
  Burdett; and now that he had forsaken his old party and joined Peel’s,
  the “tops” magnanimously decided to forsake him, and force him to
  take to—Wellingtons. We have been favoured with a report of the
  conversation that took place upon the occasion, and may perhaps
  indulge our readers with a copy of it next week.
P In the mean time, we beg to subjoin a few lines, suggested by the
  circumstance of Burdett taking the chair at Rous’s feast, which strongly
  remind us of Byron’s Vision of Belshazzar.
V Burdett was in the chair—
V The Tories throng’d the hall—
V A thousand lamps were there,
V O’er that mad festival.
V His crystal cup contain’d
V The grape-blood of the Rhine;
V Draught after draught he drain’d,
V To drown his thoughts in wine.
V In that same hour and hall
V A shade like “Glory” came,
V And wrote upon the wall
V The records of his shame.
V And at its fingers traced
V The words, as with a wand,
V The traitorous and debased
V Upraised his palsied hand.
V And in his chair he shook,
V And could no more rejoice;
V All bloodless wax’d his look,
V And tremulous his voice.
V “What words are those appear,
V To mar my fancied mirth!
V What bringeth ‘Glory’ here
V To tell of faded worth?”
V “False renegade! thy name
V Was once the star which led
V The free; but, oh! what shame
V Encircles now thine head!
V Thou’rt in the balance weigh’d,
V And worthless found at last.
V All! all! thou hast betray’d!”—
V And so the spirit pass’d.
